        "Kam-Biu Luk",
        "Koichiro Nishikawa",
        "Yifang Wang"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Establishing a nonzero third neutrino mixing angle completed a key part of the neutrino mixing picture and opened the way to important tests of neutrino properties.",
        "attribution": "The discovery depended on large collaborations; the proposed names represent leading contributions across the experiments, but individual credit is inherently limited.",
        "maturity": "The result has been confirmed by multiple experiments and is now a well-established part of particle physics.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The longlist's small set of names cannot fully represent the experimental teams responsible for the measurements."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B021",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Immanuel Bloch",
        "Juan Ignacio Cirac",
        "Markus Greiner"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Ultracold atoms in optical lattices made controllable quantum many-body systems accessible and allowed direct study of phenomena otherwise difficult to isolate.",
        "attribution": "Bloch and Greiner made major experimental advances, while Cirac helped establish the theoretical framework for quantum simulation with these systems.",
        "maturity": "The platform has been reproduced across laboratories and has supported sustained progress in probing many-body physics.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The work is a broad program with important contributions from several groups, so a three-person attribution may omit central contributors."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B003",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Alan H. Guth",
        "Andrei Linde",
        "Viatcheslav Mukhanov"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Inflation offers a deep framework for understanding the early universe and the origin of structure, with lasting influence across cosmology and fundamental physics.",
        "attribution": "Guth and Linde developed influential inflationary models, while Mukhanov made seminal contributions connecting quantum fluctuations to structure formation.",
        "maturity": "The framework is strongly integrated into modern cosmology, but its specific physical realization remains unsettled.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "There is no unique confirmed inflationary model, and the quantum-origin account is not directly established."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B044",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Michael Berry",
        "Yakir Aharonov"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry's geometric phase reveal that quantum phases carry observable physical information, with broad consequences across physics.",
        "attribution": "Aharonov's work established the effect of electromagnetic potentials on quantum phase; Berry identified a general geometric phase in adiabatic evolution.",
        "maturity": "Both ideas have extensive experimental support and have become standard tools in quantum physics.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "These are distinct contributions from different periods, and grouping them as one discovery may obscure their separate histories and wider antecedents."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
